  "context_pill": "Presumptive Taxation vs. Books of Account",
  "example_summary": "Ms. Janani, an interior decorator with ₹ 47.8L receipts, must decide between the 50% presumptive profit scheme (Section 44ADA) and maintaining books of account, considering house property income and advance tax interest.",
  "source_provision": "### Master Statutory Summary Matrix\n\n| Area / Provision | Statutory Section | Operative Threshold / Condition | Tax Treatment / Legal Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Presumptive Profession** | Section 44ADA | Gross receipts up to ₹ 75,00,000 (with cash receipts up to 5%) | Deemed profit is 50% of gross receipts; all deductions u/s 30 to 38 are deemed allowed. |\n| **Books & Audit** | Section 44AA & 44AB | Declaring lower than 50% profit | Mandatory maintenance of books and tax audit; due date is 31st October. |\n| **House Property** | Section 24 | Standard deduction on Let-out property | 30% deduction from Annual Value; self-occupied property income is Nil. |\n| **Advance Tax Interest** | Section 234B & 234C | Default in payment of advance tax installments | Simple interest @ 1% per month for default or short-payment of advance tax. |\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Presumptive Disallowance: Under Section 44ADA, once presumptive income is computed at 50%, no separate deduction for depreciation u/s 32 or any other business expense is permissible.\n\n\n> [!WARNING] Lower Profit Trap: If an assessee claims profits lower than 50% and total income exceeds the basic exemption limit, maintaining books u/s 44AA and getting an audit done u/s 44AB is compulsory.",
